Negative control for the ADD-SESSION channel (R6-2 · R6-2b · R6-8 · R6-8b).

Puts the overlay back to what the operator drove live: "Add another client"
opens, the journey renders — and collects nothing, because it was booted on the
SHARED `auth:<actor>` instance, which short-circuits `checking → authenticated`
whenever a session of that scope is already live. The pool can then only ever
shrink. `.fresh()` is the channel that skips that probe, and the marker the
funnel's target carries is the only thing that asks for it.

The mutant keeps EVERYTHING else intact: the reused `useAuth` journey, the actor
the entry named, the key, both entrances, the close on resolve. A spec asserting
"the overlay renders the journey", "the staff entry opens at staff", or "the gate
shows a login" stays GREEN under it — none of them was ever testing that a second
session can actually be collected beside a live one.

Reds: auth-overlay.spec.ts — the case(s) that seed a session of the target scope
into the pool, enter on the add-session marker, and demand the journey be booted
FRESH (the `fresh` prop reaching `AuthJourney`, or the login form still rendering
with that session live).

Re-cut: the gate's own arrival chooser now sits beside the journey (`R7-1`), so
the context around the mutated line moved again. The mutation itself is
unchanged.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

--- a/playgrounds/labs-nuxt/app/pages/overlays/auth.vue
+++ b/playgrounds/labs-nuxt/app/pages/overlays/auth.vue
@@ -10,7 +10,7 @@
       v-if="actor"
       :key="journey"
       :actor="actor"
-      :fresh="isAddSession"
+      :fresh="false"
       @logout="emit('close')"
       @resolve="handoff()"
     />
